1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,476
Welcome to chapter thirteen of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,476 --> 00:00:14,941
Today we turn a generalist model into a specialist: instruction data, loss masking, and LoRA adapters built from scratch.

3
00:00:14,941 --> 00:00:28,793
I ran the notebook, found why it can't show any improvement, and then ran the same recipe on a properly pretrained GPT-2 with a real held-out test set, so you can see what fine-tuning actually does.

4
00:00:29,643 --> 00:00:34,271
Chapter twelve was pretraining: predict the next token of raw text.

5
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:41,039
Today is fine-tuning: teach the model to answer in a specific way using question and answer pairs.

6
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:46,219
Chapter fourteen gets better behaviour without touching the weights at all.

7
00:00:47,069 --> 00:00:47,745
The plan.

8
00:00:47,745 --> 00:00:49,620
When to fine tune at all.

9
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:51,270
How to shape the data.

10
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:52,245
Loss masking.

11
00:00:52,245 --> 00:00:54,721
LoRA, from the maths to the code.

12
00:00:54,721 --> 00:00:55,921
How to evaluate.

13
00:00:55,921 --> 00:00:57,496
How to ship adapters.

14
00:00:57,496 --> 00:01:03,197
And then my two runs: the notebook as written, and a fixed version on GPT-2.

15
00:01:04,048 --> 00:01:04,826
Key terms.

16
00:01:04,826 --> 00:01:09,185
Supervised fine tuning trains on input and output pairs.

17
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:11,988
Loss masking grades only the answer.

18
00:01:11,988 --> 00:01:16,581
LoRA learns a small low rank change next to frozen weights.

19
00:01:16,581 --> 00:01:19,617
An adapter is a small trainable add on.

20
00:01:19,617 --> 00:01:22,965
The rank is the width of LoRA's bottleneck.

21
00:01:22,965 --> 00:01:26,234
And freezing means a weight never updates.

22
00:01:27,084 --> 00:01:27,665
Part one.

23
00:01:27,665 --> 00:01:29,601
Do you even need to fine tune?

24
00:01:30,451 --> 00:01:32,755
A pretrained model is a generalist.

25
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:35,782
It knows language but hasn't mastered any job.

26
00:01:35,782 --> 00:01:40,718
Ask a Shakespeare model about your refund policy and you get thee and thou.

27
00:01:40,718 --> 00:01:42,034
You have two levers.

28
00:01:42,034 --> 00:01:49,865
Give it better instructions, which is prompting, or show it many examples of the right behaviour, which is fine tuning.

29
00:01:50,715 --> 00:01:57,096
Prompting wins for simple, varied tasks, when you have no examples and need results today.

30
00:01:57,096 --> 00:02:07,376
Fine tuning wins when you need the same format and vocabulary every time, on one high volume task, and you have at least a hundred good examples.

31
00:02:08,226 --> 00:02:09,517
The rule of thumb.

32
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:12,387
Try five to ten prompt variations first.

33
00:02:12,387 --> 00:02:14,180
Then ask three questions.

34
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:17,409
Do I have a hundred or more correct examples?

35
00:02:17,409 --> 00:02:20,637
Must the model use specific terms every time?

36
00:02:20,637 --> 00:02:23,865
Is the task far from what the base model saw?

37
00:02:23,865 --> 00:02:25,945
Two or more yeses, fine tune.

38
00:02:25,945 --> 00:02:28,599
Otherwise keep working on the prompt.

39
00:02:29,449 --> 00:02:29,808
Cost.

40
00:02:29,808 --> 00:02:36,682
Prompting is fast and free to set up, but long instructions cost tokens on every single request.

41
00:02:36,682 --> 00:02:40,692
Fine tuning costs compute once, then prompts stay short.

42
00:02:40,692 --> 00:02:42,697
At high volume that can win.

43
00:02:42,697 --> 00:02:48,927
The hidden cost is that every fine tune is a new artifact to store, version and deploy.

44
00:02:49,777 --> 00:02:50,491
Part two.

45
00:02:50,491 --> 00:02:51,206
The data.

46
00:02:52,056 --> 00:02:54,896
In chapter seven a record was just text.

47
00:02:54,896 --> 00:02:58,942
Now each record is an instruction and the ideal response.

48
00:02:58,942 --> 00:03:04,835
Pretraining is like reading the whole textbook; fine tuning is drilling flashcards.

49
00:03:05,685 --> 00:03:10,020
The dataset is an FAQ for a fictional company called TechStartup.

50
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:14,288
The book says eighty training examples and twenty test examples.

51
00:03:14,288 --> 00:03:21,158
The notebook actually has sixty nine, and it takes the first eighty for training, which is all of them.

52
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:25,092
So the test set is empty, and that breaks evaluation later.

53
00:03:25,092 --> 00:03:26,293
Keep that in mind.

54
00:03:27,143 --> 00:03:30,608
JSON lines stores one JSON object per line.

55
00:03:30,608 --> 00:03:34,153
You can stream it, append to it and diff it.

56
00:03:34,153 --> 00:03:39,068
Saving is a loop of json dumps; loading reads each line back.

57
00:03:39,918 --> 00:03:45,074
In fine tuning, each example is seen many times, so errors get amplified.

58
00:03:45,074 --> 00:03:47,404
One wrong answer becomes a habit.

59
00:03:47,404 --> 00:03:56,939
Check that answers are accurate, use consistent terms, never contradict each other, cover varied phrasings, and contain nothing unsafe.

60
00:03:56,939 --> 00:04:02,660
If one record says Amsterdam and another says Berlin, the model learns confusion.

61
00:04:03,509 --> 00:04:04,331
Part three.

62
00:04:04,331 --> 00:04:05,301
Loss masking.

63
00:04:06,151 --> 00:04:09,028
In chapter twelve every position was graded.

64
00:04:09,028 --> 00:04:20,667
For question answer data, that means grading the model on predicting the words of the question itself, which teaches nothing useful and even encourages it to parrot the question.

65
00:04:20,667 --> 00:04:23,282
We only care about producing the answer.

66
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:25,170
Here's the idea.

67
00:04:25,170 --> 00:04:31,786
The prompt tokens, from the instruction marker to the closing marker, get the label minus one hundred.

68
00:04:31,786 --> 00:04:33,991
Only the answer tokens are graded.

69
00:04:33,991 --> 00:04:38,596
Like a teacher who doesn't mark you for copying the question correctly.

70
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:51,970
In code: format the prompt with the markers, tokenize prompt plus response, tokenize the prompt alone to find where the answer starts, then set everything before that to minus one hundred.

71
00:04:51,970 --> 00:05:01,163
In the notebook, one example had a hundred and fifteen masked positions and only thirteen trained ones, because the padding is masked too.

72
00:05:02,014 --> 00:05:08,346
The loss is chapter twelve's shifted cross entropy with ignore index minus one hundred.

73
00:05:08,346 --> 00:05:11,330
The training loop is the same five steps.

74
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:18,899
One tip the chapter skips: put an end of text token after each answer, so the model learns when to stop.

75
00:05:18,899 --> 00:05:20,574
My GPT-2 run does that.

76
00:05:21,424 --> 00:05:22,547
Part four.

77
00:05:22,547 --> 00:05:23,109
LoRA.

78
00:05:23,959 --> 00:05:32,273
Full fine tuning needs gradients and optimizer state for every weight, and you ship a full copy of the model for every task.

79
00:05:32,273 --> 00:05:35,089
At billions of weights that's impractical.

80
00:05:35,089 --> 00:05:41,793
And updating everything risks catastrophic forgetting, where the new task overwrites the old skills.

81
00:05:42,644 --> 00:05:47,209
LoRA's insight is that the change a fine tune needs is low rank.

82
00:05:47,209 --> 00:05:54,770
So freeze the big matrix W and learn a thin correction made of two small matrices, scaled by alpha over r.

83
00:05:54,770 --> 00:06:04,043
On a two fifty six square layer with rank eight, that's four thousand ninety six trainable numbers instead of sixty five thousand.

84
00:06:04,043 --> 00:06:05,470
Sixteen times fewer.

85
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:09,142
The book's headline is forty eight times.

86
00:06:09,142 --> 00:06:12,514
That's true for a seven sixty eight square layer.

87
00:06:12,514 --> 00:06:20,222
MiniGPT's real attention projection goes from two fifty six to seven sixty eight, which gives twenty four times.

88
00:06:20,222 --> 00:06:22,975
The notebook's demo layer gives sixteen.

89
00:06:22,975 --> 00:06:28,550
The ratio depends on the shape, so don't quote forty eight as a property of LoRA.

90
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:31,815
The scale is alpha divided by rank.

91
00:06:31,815 --> 00:06:34,507
Alpha is a volume knob for the adapter.

92
00:06:34,507 --> 00:06:44,376
Dividing by rank means that if you try a different rank, the update size stays roughly the same, so you don't need to retune the learning rate.

93
00:06:44,376 --> 00:06:47,896
Rank eight with alpha sixteen gives a scale of two.

94
00:06:48,746 --> 00:06:50,408
Here's the book's layer.

95
00:06:50,408 --> 00:06:54,566
A frozen base linear layer, two small matrices, and a scale.

96
00:06:54,566 --> 00:07:05,514
One matrix gets a small random start; the other starts at zero, so the adapter adds exactly nothing at step zero and training grows the correction from there.

97
00:07:06,364 --> 00:07:07,468
A naming gotcha.

98
00:07:07,468 --> 00:07:11,677
The book calls the down projection B and the up projection A.

99
00:07:11,677 --> 00:07:20,234
The original paper and Hugging Face's PEFT library do the opposite: A is down and starts random, B is up and starts at zero.

100
00:07:20,234 --> 00:07:26,583
The maths is the same, but if you ever load or compare PEFT weights, the names are reversed.

101
00:07:26,583 --> 00:07:30,171
The notebook shows lora B as two fifty six by eight.

102
00:07:31,022 --> 00:07:44,580
Injecting LoRA: freeze every parameter, then in every block replace the combined query key value projection with a LoRA version that copies the old weights into its frozen base.

103
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:47,491
Only the new small matrices can learn.

104
00:07:48,341 --> 00:07:54,017
The book says adapting the fused projection primarily affects queries and values.

105
00:07:54,017 --> 00:07:54,788
It doesn't.

106
00:07:54,788 --> 00:08:00,113
One adapter on the fused output can change queries, keys and values equally.

107
00:08:00,113 --> 00:08:04,949
If you want only Q and V, split the projection or mask the key slice.

108
00:08:04,949 --> 00:08:09,504
The paper's finding was about which matrices you choose to adapt.

109
00:08:10,354 --> 00:08:19,860
In the notebook, thirty two thousand seven hundred and sixty eight parameters are trainable out of sixteen million, which is zero point two percent.

110
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:24,484
The book prints a different pair of numbers from an older configuration.

111
00:08:24,484 --> 00:08:28,146
Either way only a fifth of a percent of the model learns.

112
00:08:28,996 --> 00:08:41,848
QLoRA keeps the frozen base in four bit precision and trains LoRA on top, which is how people fine tune seven billion parameter models on one consumer GPU.

113
00:08:41,848 --> 00:08:47,321
It's the same idea plus quantisation, using bitsandbytes and PEFT.

114
00:08:48,171 --> 00:08:48,927
Part five.

115
00:08:48,927 --> 00:08:49,835
Did it work?

116
00:08:50,685 --> 00:08:51,587
Three checks.

117
00:08:51,587 --> 00:08:53,599
Exact match, which is strict.

118
00:08:53,599 --> 00:08:57,415
Word overlap, the share of expected words that show up.

119
00:08:57,415 --> 00:09:02,133
And actually reading the outputs, because numbers can hide nonsense.

120
00:09:02,133 --> 00:09:05,810
All on held out questions the model never trained on.

121
00:09:06,660 --> 00:09:12,544
The book's evaluation calls model dot generate with Hugging Face style arguments.

122
00:09:12,544 --> 00:09:14,941
MiniGPT doesn't have that method.

123
00:09:14,941 --> 00:09:20,607
You need a small greedy loop: take the argmax, append it, stop at end of text.

124
00:09:20,607 --> 00:09:24,239
And set seeds before both training and evaluation.

125
00:09:25,089 --> 00:09:26,718
Here's my notebook run.

126
00:09:26,718 --> 00:09:30,968
The base model is randomly initialised, so it knows nothing.

127
00:09:30,968 --> 00:09:36,564
Loss moved from ten point eight six to ten point seven four, about one percent.

128
00:09:36,564 --> 00:09:39,398
Outputs were gibberish before and after.

129
00:09:39,398 --> 00:09:44,427
And because the test set is empty, the evaluation cell divides by zero.

130
00:09:44,427 --> 00:09:50,732
LoRA can't teach language to a random model; it can only nudge one that already knows it.

131
00:09:51,582 --> 00:09:54,977
The part that does work in the notebook: file size.

132
00:09:54,977 --> 00:10:03,700
The adapter is a hundred and thirty two kilobytes, the full model sixty one megabytes, four hundred and seventy eight times bigger.

133
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:08,627
The book's numbers come from an older configuration, but the point stands.

134
00:10:09,477 --> 00:10:12,082
So I ran the same recipe properly.

135
00:10:12,082 --> 00:10:29,402
A pretrained GPT-2 small, LoRA rank eight alpha sixteen on every attention projection, the book's sixty nine FAQ examples shuffled into fifty five for training and fourteen held out, with loss masking and an end of text token.

136
00:10:30,252 --> 00:10:31,654
Here are my numbers.

137
00:10:31,654 --> 00:10:37,614
Training loss fell from three point one five to point seven three over twelve epochs.

138
00:10:37,614 --> 00:10:46,519
On the fourteen held out questions, exact match stayed at zero, and word overlap rose from five percent to eighteen and a half.

139
00:10:46,519 --> 00:10:53,320
On questions it trained on, overlap was fifty three percent, with three of fourteen word perfect.

140
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:59,911
Only a quarter of a percent of GPT-2's weights trained, and the adapter is about one megabyte.

141
00:10:59,911 --> 00:11:10,780
So the book's fifty percent exact match isn't realistic for unseen questions: with sixty nine examples, the model learns the style, not facts it never saw.

142
00:11:11,630 --> 00:11:14,161
Reading the answers tells the real story.

143
00:11:14,161 --> 00:11:20,891
Where are you based: Amsterdam, correct, because other training answers mention it, though it repeats itself.

144
00:11:20,891 --> 00:11:24,904
Customer service: it invents an email address and a phone number.

145
00:11:24,904 --> 00:11:29,164
Uptime: a vague non answer instead of ninety nine point nine percent.

146
00:11:29,164 --> 00:11:35,708
The fine tune learned the company's voice and answer format perfectly, but facts it never saw get made up.

147
00:11:35,708 --> 00:11:40,771
Fine tuning teaches behaviour; for facts, use retrieval, which is chapter fifteen.

148
00:11:41,621 --> 00:11:42,357
Part six.

149
00:11:42,357 --> 00:11:43,093
Shipping.

150
00:11:43,943 --> 00:11:47,338
Save only the parameters whose names contain lora.

151
00:11:47,338 --> 00:11:57,456
To use it, build the same model, add the same LoRA structure, and load with strict equals false, because the base weights aren't in the adapter file.

152
00:11:57,456 --> 00:12:02,482
Remember the base must be the same pretrained weights you trained against.

153
00:12:03,332 --> 00:12:07,267
Keep adapters separate if you want to swap tasks like apps.

154
00:12:07,267 --> 00:12:13,603
Merge if you want one simple file and slightly faster inference, accepting that it's permanent.

155
00:12:13,603 --> 00:12:19,673
Merging is just adding the scaled product into W, transposed for the linear layer's layout.

156
00:12:20,523 --> 00:12:21,254
Takeaways.

157
00:12:21,254 --> 00:12:25,933
Prompt first, fine tune when you have the data and a clear need.

158
00:12:25,933 --> 00:12:28,565
Loss masking grades only the answer.

159
00:12:28,565 --> 00:12:32,440
LoRA trains a tiny low rank update on frozen weights.

160
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:42,091
And the lesson from my runs: fine tuning only works on a pretrained base, and you only know it worked with a real held out test set.

161
00:12:42,941 --> 00:12:43,941
Short answers.

162
00:12:43,941 --> 00:12:49,156
Two hundred examples and a fixed format: fine tune, after trying prompts.

163
00:12:49,156 --> 00:12:51,157
Masking stops wasted signal.

164
00:12:51,157 --> 00:12:57,301
LoRA works because task changes are low rank, though bigger shifts may need more rank.

165
00:12:57,301 --> 00:12:59,372
Freezing prevents forgetting.

166
00:12:59,372 --> 00:13:04,445
And ten thousand noisy examples can be worse than a hundred clean ones.

167
00:13:05,295 --> 00:13:06,849
The checkpoint, fixed.

168
00:13:06,849 --> 00:13:08,756
Load trained weights first.

169
00:13:08,756 --> 00:13:12,642
Shuffle and split the data so the test set isn't empty.

170
00:13:12,642 --> 00:13:16,951
Rank eight, end of text on answers, a greedy evaluation loop.

171
00:13:16,951 --> 00:13:22,532
Report exact match, overlap, three before and after pairs and the adapter size.

172
00:13:23,382 --> 00:13:23,990
Practice.

173
00:13:23,990 --> 00:13:27,437
Sweep the rank and plot overlap against parameters.

174
00:13:27,437 --> 00:13:31,694
Train once without masking and see if it parrots the questions.

175
00:13:31,694 --> 00:13:36,289
And train a second adapter for another domain and swap between them.

176
00:13:37,139 --> 00:13:41,799
This video is based on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

177
00:13:41,799 --> 00:13:52,546
The explanations and diagrams are my own, but the book goes deeper, so please support the author and buy it at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

178
00:13:52,546 --> 00:13:57,732
The code is on GitHub at First LLM slash code, under the MIT licence.

179
00:13:57,732 --> 00:14:01,715
Thanks for watching, and see you in chapter fourteen.

